+# Museum collections policy: more precise custody and preservation records
+
+**November 4, 2026.** The restored, verified Intel share enabled a full visual reading of the museum’s **32-page collections policy**. The useful advance is a map to specific underlying records: temporary-custody and accession entries, donor and loan files, acquisition minutes, earlier conservation reports, source-image associations, digital-ingest checksums, and permanently retained deaccession records. None of those object-specific records was recovered in this review.
+
+This refines **STL01–STL03 / AUD011 / AUD016 / B188 / H005** and the earlier **CONS-POL-01, FBI-STL-01, CONS-STL-01/02** retrieval leads. It completes the previously deferred policy-page reading; **all eighteen original investigation requirements remain open or partial**. The policy does not authenticate any steel specimen, establish WTC 7 origin, supply a forensic result or show misconduct.
+
+## Source and dates actually read
+
+[The institutional PDF](https://www.911memorial.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/Collections%20Mgmt%20July%202024.pdf) is **5,342,355 bytes**, SHA-256 **`ba008fdd818f2bfae73598c41c32193779d1e891a2cc2c0590dbe157dcaa36e1`**, acquired November 4, 2026. Its bytes are unchanged. It has an unnumbered contents page followed by printed pages **1–31**; PDF physical page = printed page + 1.
+
+The closing statement, [physical 32 / printed 31](https://www.911memorial.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/Collections%20Mgmt%20July%202024.pdf#page=32), states adoption **April 17, 2007**, followed by revisions **July 28, 2011; October 17, 2013; May 3, 2016; November 15, 2018; and March 27, 2024**. These are dates printed in the captured document, not independently authenticated Board minutes or native approval history. The filename’s **July 2024** label is a separate observation; it does not establish an approval or publication date. Printed page 2’s **June 26, 2007** statement concerns adoption of the mission statement, rather than the whole policy.
+
+Earlier editions, actual revision/approval records and posting history remain missing. This later document cannot establish what rule governed a 2001 recovery or the reported 2010 FBI transfer. A five-year review provision also does not identify the date of every review; revision dates alone cannot prove a missed review.
+
