+The numbering provision, **physical 15 / printed 14, III.9**, describes year/lot/item numbering and an annual ledger. A registration-year field should not silently become a recovery, construction or damage date. This general rule does not itself identify the provenance of a previously discussed accession.
+
+## Important distinctions and contrary evidence
+
+The **City-property reclassification account** is a useful reason to distinguish deaccession from physical destruction. The document describes an administrative ownership/status change with continued custody; the actual item lists and agreements remain unacquired. That account does not decide the fate of any other missing artifact. The separate witnessed-destruction provisions remain preserved alongside it.
+
+The policy also distinguishes accessioned collections from reference/study/tribute/library holdings and non-collection props or reproductions (**physical 3, 9–10 and 16 / printed 2, 8–9 and 15**). A missing deed or accession number has to be assessed against the item’s actual category. No displayed artifact is newly characterized as a prop or a reproduction.
+
+Protective provisions are material counterevidence to an assumption that conservation necessarily erased damage: the treatment section calls for preserving damaged appearance, documentation and authorized intervention. Conversely, a stated safeguard does not prove compliance. The relevant object’s before/after records, dates and applicable policy are the discriminating evidence. A policy, inventory, custody sheet and disposal memorandum about one item are not independent confirmations of wrongdoing.
+
+The document’s **Forms** section (**physical 32 / printed 31, XI**) describes forms being created, updated and available on request. **No actual form templates or completed object files are included in this 32-page PDF.** Earlier templates, completed records, earlier policy versions, the separate digital-preservation and Operations and Maintenance plans, and actual implementation evidence remain TODOs. Nothing was requested from a person or agency.
+
+## Preserved conflicts and unproved joins
+
+The earlier **180 million versus 1.8 million tons** public descriptions, tentative **elevator/rail** origin of the FBI artifact, museum tower/floor descriptions and unjoined steel identifiers remain unchanged. This policy supplies no statistical source worksheet or artifact-level join that adjudicates them. No museum-to-FBI, City-to-Port Authority, NIST-to-museum or WTC 7 identity is established.
+
+A record target is not an acquired record, a policy promise is not implementation, and administrative retention is not a laboratory result. The museum’s policy is not applied to the FBI or other holders as though it governed their files. No historical cause, intelligence affiliation, concealment, unlawful destruction or demolition conclusion is supported by this review.
+
+## Actual reading, preservation and publication scope
+
+All **32 complete rendered pages** were visually read, including the closing date/signature area. No contact sheet, search snippet or OCR output substitutes for that reading. The source has no ordinary extractable text; the earlier zero-text and offline-share observations remain preserved. The restored verified media store enabled the review, rather than those observations being rewritten.
+
+Existing Poppler rendered the unchanged PDF at **144 dpi** to **32 new Intel-store PNGs** totaling **31,052,008 bytes**, with per-page hashes and media-guard receipts. The public payload contains original notes and integrity/page locators only: **no source PDF, page images, forms, signature/name transcription, donor/contact data, private case/correspondence/strategy or credentials**. No physical, chemical, audio, independent-clock or original-custody test was performed.
